  "passages": [
    {
      "id": "P1",
      "title": "Thandi and the Red Bicycle",
      "text": "Thandi has a red bicycle. Every Saturday she rides it to the shop with her brother, Sipho. They buy bread and milk. Then they ride home together."
    },
    {
      "id": "P2",
      "title": "Rain in the Karoo",
      "text": "The Karoo is a dry region in South Africa. Farmers there watch the sky carefully, because rain falls for only a short time each year. When the rain finally comes, dry riverbeds fill with water and small plants bloom quickly across the veld. Within a few weeks the flowers fade, and the land becomes dry again."
    },
    {
      "id": "P3",
      "title": "The Library Fine",
      "text": "The school library has changed its late-return policy. Before, learners paid a fine of R2 for every day a book was overdue. Now they help shelve books for one hour instead. Mr Dlamini, the librarian, says this helps learners understand how books are organised. He also says the fine often stopped poorer learners from borrowing at all."
    },
    {
      "id": "P4",
      "title": "Checking the News",
      "text": "Many people assume a newspaper report is accurate simply because it is printed. However, a careful reader checks whether the report names its sources, whether its claims can be verified, and whether the writer has a stake in the outcome. A report that fails these checks should be treated as an opinion rather than as fact."
    },
    {
      "id": "P5",
      "title": "The Ripple Effect",
      "text": "Economists sometimes describe a multiplier effect. When a town's largest employer closes, the loss spreads beyond the lost jobs. Shops lose customers, suppliers lose orders, and families spend less, which can cost other workers their jobs too. The effect is strongest in small towns, where few other employers exist to take on displaced workers."
    }
  ],
